  "modules": [
    {
      "name": "Buffers",
      "source": "Src/Buffers.rux",
      "header": "Compressing and decompressing whole buffers, for data already in memory.\nBoth run the streaming reader and writer over a source that reads a slice and a sink that appends to storage this\nfunction owns. An interface value holds its own copy of what it was made from, so the sink writes through the\nstorage's address, and the function collects the bytes from there when the stream is done."
    },
    {
      "name": "Deflate",
      "source": "Src/Deflate.rux",
      "header": "Writing data as a compressed stream.\nData is gathered until the buffer is full, then encoded as one block, with the 32 KiB before it kept so that\nmatches can reach back into earlier blocks. Repeats are found through hash chains on three-byte prefixes, as zlib\nfinds them; the higher levels follow longer chains and look one byte ahead before taking a match, in case a longer\none starts there. Each block is then written whichever way is smallest: stored as it is, with the fixed codes, or\nwith codes built for that block's own frequencies.\nNothing is complete until `Finish` writes the final block and the container's trailer, so a writer dropped without\nfinishing leaves a stream a decompressor reports as truncated."
    },
    {
      "name": "Format",
      "source": "Src/Format.rux",
      "header": "The three containers a Deflate stream travels in, how hard to compress, and the tables RFC 1951 fixes."
    },
    {
      "name": "Huffman",
      "source": "Src/Huffman.rux",
      "header": "The canonical Huffman codes of RFC 1951: reading them from code lengths, and choosing lengths for a set of\nfrequencies.\nA code is described entirely by the length of each symbol's code word, so both directions work from lengths.\nDecoding counts the codes of each length and walks the lengths one bit at a time, as zlib's `puff` does: no table to\nbuild beyond the counts and the symbols in code order. Encoding builds an ordinary Huffman tree, then limits its\ndepth the way zlib does, moving the deepest leaves up and splitting a shallower one until every code fits, and\nhands the lengths out again with the shortest to the most frequent symbols."
    },
    {
      "name": "Inflate",
      "source": "Src/Inflate.rux",
      "header": "Reading a compressed stream back as the data it holds.\nThe decompressor pulls compressed bytes from its source as it needs them and stops only when the caller's buffer is\nfull, so it is a state machine: between reads it remembers which part of the stream it is in, how much of a stored\nblock is left, and how much of a match it has still to copy. The last 32 KiB it produced are kept, since a match\nmay reach back that far. The container's checksum is computed over everything produced and checked against the\ntrailer before the end of the data is reported, so a reader that reached the end got the data that was compressed."
    }
  ],
